<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>CogStim is an open-source Python package for generating visual
  stimuli used in numerical cognition, psychophysics and computer vision
  research. It produces dot arrays for numerosity perception tasks,
  geometric shapes, oriented stripe patterns and fixation targets, and
  writes them as standard image files that any experiment software or
  machine-learning pipeline can load. The package is designed so that
  researchers can easily set every parameter of a stimulus set to meet
  the requirements of their study, from the number, size and colour of
  the elements to the conditions the set is balanced across and the
  correlated visual properties, such as total surface, figure placement,
  or convex hull. Every run records its parameters and random seed, so a
  set can be regenerated exactly and exported for model training. The
  package can be used from the command line or as a Python library.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>Many experiments in psychology, neuroscience and vision research
  need large sets of images whose visual properties are controlled: how
  many items each image holds, their size, position and colour. In
  numerosity tasks, for instance, adding dots also increases the surface
  they cover and the area they spread over, and participants can use
  those cues instead of number
  (<xref alt="Gebuis &amp; Reynvoet, 2011" rid="ref-gebuis_generating_2011" ref-type="bibr">Gebuis
  &amp; Reynvoet, 2011</xref>). Laboratories usually write their own
  scripts for this. Such scripts are rarely published, the controls they
  apply are hard to state precisely, and their stimuli cannot be
  regenerated once the script is lost, which makes results hard to
  compare across studies and across model evaluations.</p>
  <p>To make such stimuli easy to produce and to reproduce, we built
  CogStim, which generates them offline, as ordinary image files, for
  the paradigms our own work needed: two-colour dot arrays for the
  Approximate Number System (ANS)
  (<xref alt="Halberda et al., 2008" rid="ref-halberda_individual_2008" ref-type="bibr">Halberda
  et al., 2008</xref>), sample and match pairs for enumeration
  (<xref alt="Sella et al., 2013" rid="ref-sella_enumeration_2013" ref-type="bibr">Sella
  et al., 2013</xref>), single-colour dot arrays, and the simpler
  stimuli that accompany them in a test battery or a model benchmark:
  geometric shapes, oriented stripes
  (<xref alt="Srinivasan, 2021" rid="ref-srinivasan_vision_2021" ref-type="bibr">Srinivasan,
  2021</xref>) and fixation targets
  (<xref alt="Thaler et al., 2013" rid="ref-thaler_what_2013" ref-type="bibr">Thaler
  et al., 2013</xref>). Dots never overlap or leave the canvas, and the
  total surface of the two arrays in a pair can be left random, matched,
  or made to contradict the numerical ratio. Experimenters load the PNG,
  JPEG or SVG files into presentation software such as PsychoPy or
  jsPsych. Researchers who train or evaluate vision models can also
  request a CSV or JSON file giving the position, bounding box, size and
  colour of every element in every image.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>Experiments in behavioural research are usually built with an
  experiment engine: PsychoPy
  (<xref alt="Peirce et al., 2019" rid="ref-peirce_psychopy2_2019" ref-type="bibr">Peirce
  et al., 2019</xref>) in Python, Psychtoolbox
  (<xref alt="Brainard, 1997" rid="ref-brainard_psychophysics_1997" ref-type="bibr">Brainard,
  1997</xref>) in MATLAB, jsPsych
  (<xref alt="Leeuw, 2015" rid="ref-de_leeuw_jspsych_2015" ref-type="bibr">Leeuw,
  2015</xref>) in the web browser, and PsyToolkit
  (<xref alt="Stoet, 2010" rid="ref-stoet_psytoolkit_2010" ref-type="bibr">Stoet,
  2010</xref>,
  <xref alt="2017" rid="ref-stoet_psytoolkit_2017" ref-type="bibr">2017</xref>).
  All of them can draw dot arrays, shapes and gratings, and they draw
  them while the experiment runs, inside the trial loop. That is what
  precise timing, adaptive procedures and gaze-contingent displays
  require, but it also ties each stimulus to the engine and the
  experiment that draws it. The images are usually drawn anew in each
  session, so checking them before participants see them means running
  the experiment or adding code to capture the screen, and the set as a
  whole is rarely inspected. Their construction is written in each
  experiment’s own code, so another study or another engine cannot reuse
  it. And any constraint that concerns the whole set, such as presenting
  every ratio equally often, has to be programmed by hand in the trial
  logic.</p>
  <p>We built CogStim as a separate tool, rather than as part of one of
  these engines, for three reasons. First, the stimuli are often needed
  outside any engine. Psychological and educational test batteries are
  increasingly run on web platforms and on tablet applications developed
  in house, both in laboratories and in schools
  (<xref alt="Anwyl-Irvine et al., 2020" rid="ref-anwyl-irvine_gorilla_2020" ref-type="bibr">Anwyl-Irvine
  et al., 2020</xref>;
  <xref alt="Bridges et al., 2020" rid="ref-bridges_timing_2020" ref-type="bibr">Bridges
  et al., 2020</xref>;
  <xref alt="Correig-Fraga et al., 2024" rid="ref-correig-fraga_development_2024" ref-type="bibr">Correig-Fraga
  et al., 2024</xref>), and the same kind of stimuli is used to train
  and evaluate neural networks
  (<xref alt="Nasr et al., 2019" rid="ref-nasr_number_2019" ref-type="bibr">Nasr
  et al., 2019</xref>;
  <xref alt="Testolin et al., 2020" rid="ref-testolin_visual_2020" ref-type="bibr">Testolin
  et al., 2020</xref>). Exporting thousands of images from an engine
  means running its window system without a screen, whereas our package
  directly writes the files. Second, a stimulus set should be checked
  before anyone sees it. Many designs need every image to be correct and
  the set as a whole to be balanced: dots that never overlap or leave
  the canvas, two arrays whose total surface is matched when the design
  requires it, and every count and ratio represented equally often.
  Because our package builds the whole set before the experiment and
  writes it to disk, these constraints are verified while the set is
  built, anything that could not be produced is reported, and the
  researcher can inspect every image, or analyse the set as a whole,
  before the first participant sees it. Third, a stimulus set should be
  part of the research record. Every run writes its options and random
  seed next to the images, so the same set can be regenerated, inspected
  and reused by other studies whatever software they present it with, as
  reproducible research requires.</p>
  <p>A second group of tools generates numerosity stimuli specifically:
  the program of Gebuis and Reynvoet
  (<xref alt="Gebuis &amp; Reynvoet, 2011" rid="ref-gebuis_generating_2011" ref-type="bibr">Gebuis
  &amp; Reynvoet, 2011</xref>), CUSTOM
  (<xref alt="De Marco &amp; Cutini, 2020" rid="ref-de_marco_introducing_2020" ref-type="bibr">De
  Marco &amp; Cutini, 2020</xref>), NASCO
  (<xref alt="Guillaume et al., 2020" rid="ref-guillaume_nasco_2020" ref-type="bibr">Guillaume
  et al., 2020</xref>) and GeNEsIS
  (<xref alt="Zanon et al., 2022" rid="ref-zanon_towards_2022" ref-type="bibr">Zanon
  et al., 2022</xref>). They control the continuous visual properties of
  dot arrays in detail and are the closest relatives of our dot tasks,
  but all four are written in MATLAB, so running or modifying them
  requires a commercial licence. By contrast, CogStim is a Python
  package: it is open source and runs on any computer with a free Python
  installation, which makes it available to any laboratory, school or
  student regardless of the software they can pay for. It also records
  each run so that it can be regenerated, exports the position of every
  element for model training, and produces the other stimuli of a
  battery, such as shapes, stripes and fixation targets, alongside the
  dot arrays.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>The software architecture prioritizes modularity and simplicity so
  that adding a stimulus type and changing stimuli details is safe and
  easy. A base generator class does everything common to all tasks: it
  builds the list of images a run will produce, creates the output
  directories, sets the random seed, saves each image in the requested
  format and records what was drawn. Each stimulus type is a subclass
  that only decides what a set contains and how one image is drawn,
  decoupling the new stimulus creation from the inner workings of the
  package.</p>
  <p>Users choose a stimulus set through options, which we designed so
  that one set of rules covers every task. Each option is declared once
  and shared by every task that uses it. Names follow an
  easy-to-understand pattern, with the unit in the name
  (<monospace>--min-dot-radius-px</monospace>) and colours numbered
  (<monospace>--dot-colour-1</monospace>), and every default is shown in
  the help. Before generating anything, each run builds the list of
  images it will produce: <monospace>--dry-run</monospace> prints how
  many there will be, and the message at the end of a run reports how
  many were actually written. Each option is declared in a registry, so
  that it cannot fall out of step with the program.</p>
  <p>For reporting and reproducibility, each run writes its options and
  seed to a <monospace>cogstim_config.yaml</monospace> file beside the
  images, and <monospace>cogstim run</monospace> takes that file as
  input. The file is both the record of a set and the way to produce it
  again: researchers who prefer configuration files can write one
  directly, and those who prefer the command line still end up with
  one.</p>
  <p>The package needs only Python 3.10 or later
  (<xref alt="Python Software Foundation, 2026" rid="ref-python" ref-type="bibr">Python
  Software Foundation, 2026</xref>), Pillow
  (<xref alt="Clark &amp; Pillow contributors, 2015" rid="ref-clark2015pillow" ref-type="bibr">Clark
  &amp; Pillow contributors, 2015</xref>) for drawing, NumPy
  (<xref alt="Harris et al., 2020" rid="ref-harris2020array" ref-type="bibr">Harris
  et al., 2020</xref>) for geometry, tqdm
  (<xref alt="Costa-Luis &amp; others, 2022" rid="ref-tqdm" ref-type="bibr">Costa-Luis
  &amp; others, 2022</xref>) for progress bars and PyYAML for
  configuration files. All of them install with
  <monospace>pip</monospace>, and none needs a display, so it runs
  unchanged on a laptop, a server or a batch job. We chose this over
  building on the drawing layer of an experiment engine, accepting that
  it cannot present stimuli or record responses.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p>Innovamat Education uses CogStim to generate the dot-array stimuli
  of COSMOS, a cognitive and numerical screening test that it
  administers twice every school year to about 50,000 children in the
  first two years of primary school. These stimuli were used in a
  published study of a response-to-intervention framework in mathematics
  (<xref alt="Correig-Fraga et al., 2024" rid="ref-correig-fraga_development_2024" ref-type="bibr">Correig-Fraga
  et al., 2024</xref>). The package also produced the synthetic image
  datasets of a study of visual computation in the Drosophila visual
  system
  (<xref alt="Correig-Fraga et al., 2026" rid="ref-correig-fraga_structure_2026" ref-type="bibr">Correig-Fraga
  et al., 2026</xref>).</p>
  <p>The code is released under the MIT licence, so any laboratory,
  school or company can use it, modify it and redistribute it, including
  inside their own applications. It is also built to be adapted to each
  researcher’s needs. Adding a stimulus type means writing one class
  that states what a set contains and how one image is drawn; file
  handling, seeding, image counts and the exports come from the base
  class, and the developer documentation works through a complete
  example. The same design serves the reproducibility that journals
  increasingly ask for
  (<xref alt="Nosek et al., 2015" rid="ref-nosek_promoting_2015" ref-type="bibr">Nosek
  et al., 2015</xref>). A study can share the configuration file of its
  stimuli rather than the images or the script that made them, and
  anyone can regenerate exactly the same set, inspect it, or change one
  parameter and generate a variant.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="example-figures">
  <title>Example figures</title>
  <fig>
    <caption><p>CogStim stimuli
    examples.<styled-content id="figU003Astimuli"></styled-content></p></caption>
    <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="stimuli_panel.png" />
  </fig>
  <p><bold>Figure 1.</bold> Example stimuli. <bold>(a, b)</bold>
  Two-colour dot arrays for ANS tasks, with dot sizes drawn
  independently (a) or with the same total surface for both colours (b).
  <bold>(c, d, i-k)</bold> Geometric shapes. <bold>(e, f)</bold>
  Single-colour dot arrays, as used in match-to-sample tasks. <bold>(g,
  h)</bold> Oriented stripes. <bold>(l)</bold> Fixation target.</p>
</sec>
